ly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}.$" 13194139535586 true] [17592186157002 1145 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{\\textrm{i}t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t + \\textrm{i}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t + 2\\textrm{i}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(\\cos t - \\sin t) + \\textrm{i}(\\cos t + \\sin t) \\\\ 2\\cos t + 2\\textrm{i}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ 2\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are l